   {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Will the transplanted hair ever fall out after 5 or 10 years?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "If the grafts are taken from the true safe donor zone, transplanted hair usually remains long-term. It may shed temporarily after surgery, then regrow. Years later, it can age naturally, but it should not disappear like DHT-sensitive bald scalp hair. If grafts are taken from unsafe areas, they may thin later."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I need to take hair loss medications forever to keep my transplanted hair?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Usually, medications are not needed to keep correctly transplanted hair alive. But they may be needed to protect your existing native hair. If your original hair is still thinning, medical management can help preserve density. The need for medication depends on your age, hair loss pattern, donor strength, and progression risk."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if my native hair keeps receding behind the permanent transplanted hairline?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "This can happen if progressive hair loss is not controlled. The transplanted hairline may remain, but the native hair behind it may thin. That can create a visible gap. This is why we plan hairlines carefully and may recommend medications, PRP, GFC, or future staged restoration when needed."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does the surgeon determine if my donor area is strong enough for a permanent result?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "We examine the density, hair caliber, scalp laxity, donor stability, family history, age, and pattern of hair loss. We also check whether the donor hair lies within the true safe zone. A responsible surgeon does not just count grafts. The surgeon studies whether those grafts are likely to remain stable for life."
      }
     }
    ],
